                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Where do I begin?\nDespite me giving this game a reccomended yes for this review, but it comes with a very big asterisk*.\nIt is in fact not the most henious otome plots I've ever encountered. Morality & the sense of security/modern sensibilities ought to be set aside if you are to run throughout all the routes. This is in fact a very *lite* otome, reminiscent of its origins as a mobile otome. As such, it is a pulp-romance fiction in its experience.\nPrincess Saya (MC) has a viable personality, so she's not too henious as a blank slate.\nConsent in this game is non-existent to specific routes; as you may have read from previous reviews, yes, there are psuedo-incest routes. But as far as pulp-fiction romance plots go, this is not as offensive in contrary to others I've seen. However.\nThe story is really lite in reading. Every route is an average of 3-4 hours reading. some endings are surprisingly melancholic/tragic. 1 raised my brows over the writer's choices. This game isn't too deep by the soul. And the only offense it held was the lack of consent on 1 or 2 of the LIs aside from the psuedo incest elements. The actual *M* rated aspects are more or less heavily implied but little to no description of its scenarios.\nThe Love Interests themselves are unfortunately not so interesting in the interpretation of the historical Japanese Sengoku period figures. Most of them aren't really *too* bad. Masamune & Kotaro at least were routes I favoured most as the tamest of the 5 LIs. Hanbei was the least and uninteresting route.\n\nI recommend buying this on a sale, play it when you're in a headspace that isn't riled up by morally dubious offences with no expectations.\nYes. the bar is low. If I had a rating system other than a yes or a no, I give this game an average of 2.8/5 or a 3/5 stars at most.",
